## Effect System (`effects/` + `pipeline/`)

### PlaygroundConfig (manifest)

Each effect is a `PlaygroundConfig` with: `name`, `canvas` size, `fields` (user-tunable uniforms with slider ranges), optional `staticUniforms`, ordered `passes[]`, and `animation.slots[]` — declarations of which animation pool indices the effect consumes (with UI labels and default min/max for the controller).

### Animation Channels (universal pool)

A fixed-size pool of `ANIMATION_CHANNEL_COUNT = 8` generic channels. Effects don't pick names; they pick **indices**. Shaders bind the corresponding `vec4 uChan{i}` uniform; the four components carry the user-controlled drive, its 0..1 form, the controller-mapped value, and the upstream state flag.

| Component | Source | Notes |
|-----------|--------|-------|
| `.x` (drive) | upstream `Signal.value` (unclamped) | `Timer mode=unbounded` grows linearly forever (good for monotonic phase / scroll); `Interpolator` profiles `sine`/`triangle`/`bell`/`gaussian`/`pulse` oscillate 0..1; a paused Timer holds a static phase. To control speed, change `Timer.durationMs`. **NOT** the upstream `Signal.time` ms field — that's intentionally hidden so the user's choice of waveform actually governs what shaders see (otherwise even a paused timer would keep ramping `time` forever and animate everything). |
| `.y` (raw) | `clamp01(Signal.value)` | 0..1 driver after clamping. Useful when the shader needs a bounded mix factor regardless of upstream mode. |
| `.z` (value) | `lerp(min, max, .y)` | Physical value mapped through the AnimationController's per-slot `min_i`/`max_i` params. Most shaders use this for amplitude-style modulation (radial offset, size, glow multiplier, intensity). |
| `.w` (state) | upstream `Signal.state` | 0 = idle, 1 = clip in progress. |

Canonical slot layout (convention only, not enforced — any shader can opt into any slot):

| Slot | Role |
|------|------|
| 0    | primary phase driver (scroll / phase / progress) |
| 1    | radial offset / phase |
| 2    | width / size |
| 3    | spacing / glow |
| 4    | intensity (alpha multiplier) |
| 5    | noise time driver (see `src/pipeline/noise.glsl.ts`) |
| 6, 7 | free |

The `EffectProcessor` warns once per (effect, slot) when a shader references an undeclared `uChan{i}` so manifest/shader drift surfaces in the console.

**Speed is upstream, period.** Effect manifests no longer expose per-uniform speed knobs (`uOrbitSpeed`, `uRaySpeed`, `uWaveSpeed`, `uNoiseSpeed`, `uSlideSpeed`, `uDropPulseSpeed`, `uColorSpeed`, `uAnimationSpeed`, etc. — all removed). Animation rate is fully owned by the chain `Timer (mode + durationMs) → optional Interpolator/Envelope/Combine → AnimationController (min/max)`. To pause an effect: set `Timer.paused = true` (the channel freezes at its current phase). To slow / speed up: tune `durationMs` on the Timer (smaller = faster `value` growth in `unbounded` mode, faster cycle in `looped`). To rescale amplitude: expand/contract the controller's `min_i`/`max_i` range for that slot.

### Pass Types

- **Fullscreen**: fragment shader rendered to a fullscreen quad. Optional `requiresInputs`.
- **Instanced**: vertex + fragment shaders with per-instance geometry from contour data. Optional `scrolling` config picks `mode` (`glyph` for one-instance-per-glyph, `ribbon` for tilted-quad strip), names the spacing/segment fields it reads, and (via `phaseSlot`, default 0) picks which animation channel's `.value` drives the contour scroll phase in pixels. There is no `speedField` — scroll speed comes from the upstream signal driving the chosen slot.

### Runtime (EffectProcessor)

Resolves active effect from `config` input, builds `UniformGroup` from fields + static uniforms + the full 8-channel animation pool (`uChan0..uChan7` as `vec4(drive, raw, value, state)`) + the full 8-slot texture pool (`uTxcn0..uTxcn7`, with a 1×1 white fallback for unconnected slots referenced by the shader), and binds the optional `source` and `coords_tex` inputs. Passes execute in order into a single output RT. Uses `Shader.from` with WebGL2 GLSL.

### Available Effects

Currently shipped (8, see `src/effects/index.ts`): `ping-pong-morphing` (single wave), `god-rays` (single ray), `light-beam` (single ray), `dot-orbit`, `dot-grid-orbit`, `text-orbit`, `text-grid-orbit`, `marching-ants`. Multi-ray / multi-wave compositions are built by **chaining multiple Effect nodes** through the `source` input (with `blend: 'add'`) — one node per ray/wave with its own colour, density, phase offset, etc. The shaders themselves render exactly one instance.

None of these effects expose a speed parameter. The configs only carry geometric / colour / shape uniforms; everything that animates is fed via animation slots and is therefore driven by the user's Timer + (optional Interpolator) + AnimationController graph.

### Texture Channels (universal pool)

In addition to the animation pool, the Effect node exposes a fixed-size pool of generic texture inputs `txcn0..txcn7` (`TEXTURE_CHANNEL_COUNT = 8`, see `pipeline/types.ts`). Each manifest declares which slots it consumes via `PlaygroundConfig.textures.slots`, with a human-readable label that the Effect node UI renders next to the corresponding handle (e.g. `txcn0 · SDF`, `txcn1 · Atlas`). Shaders bind only the channels they need by declaring `uniform sampler2D uTxcn{i};`. Slots the manifest doesn't declare still exist on the node — the user can wire any texture into them, and shaders may opt into any pool index. There is no longer a typed `sdf` / `atlas` boundary at the node level; the same Effect node can host any combination of texture maps. Unconnected `txcn{i}` referenced by a shader fall back to a 1×1 white texture.

### Noise Standard

Canonical primitives in `src/pipeline/noise.glsl.ts` (`hash21`, `valueNoise2D`, `fbm2D`) are TS-imported and concatenated into every fragment/vertex shader that needs noise. Time-driven noise reads from slot 5 — shaders sample `uv + uChan5.x * driftRate * driftDir`. Drift speed is owned upstream by the Timer wired into slot 5 (`unbounded` mode for steady drift, `paused = true` to freeze it); shaders never invent their own time source and never multiply by a per-effect speed uniform.

### Noise Visualizer (`noiseVisualizer`)

A debug surface for the canonical `fbm2D`. Renders a binary pattern (chess or stripes) sampled at `uv + noise displacement` so the user can *see* what the noise field is doing to a known surface. Two decorrelated `fbm2D` samples form a 2D vector field; amplitude is in UV units (typical 0..0.3).

- Input: `signal: SIGNAL` (typically wired from Timer, optionally through Interpolator). Drift driver = `signal.value`, NOT `signal.time` — that way a paused Timer → static field, `unbounded` → steady drift, Interpolator `sine`/`triangle` profile → breathing. Without an upstream signal `tDrive = 0` and the field is frozen.
- Output: `texture: TEXTURE`. Wire to a Preview node to inspect.
- Params: `mode` (chess/stripes), `scale` (noise frequency), `amp` (displacement), `cells`/`stripes` (pattern frequency), `speed` (drift gain).

### Effect Profiling (`log`)

`EffectProcessor.execute()` records per-pass CPU build time (mesh + shader + uniform setup) and a single GPU dispatch time around `renderer.render()`. Per-pass GPU timing isn't split because the whole stage flushes through one batched draw — true per-pass GPU timing would need `EXT_disjoint_timer_query`, which is unevenly supported. Output `metrics: METRICS` carries `EffectMetrics`.

The `log` node consumes `metrics` and stores both the raw last frame and an EMA-smoothed copy (`smoothing` param, default 0.85). The view shows `total / cpu / gpu dispatch / fps cap`, then a per-pass list with id, kind tag (`inst` for instanced, `full` for fullscreen), `cpuMs`, and a horizontal bar proportional to the heaviest pass. Skipped passes show "skipped" with no bar. React commits are throttled to 100ms regardless of upstream tick rate — the smoothed numbers don't change visibly faster than that.

Logical use: drop a Log node next to an Effect, wire `effect.metrics → log.metrics`, watch which pass dominates the budget while you tweak parameters. With the FPS cap turned down (e.g. 10fps), profiling stays cheap during exploratory work.

